<section id="section-03">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">03</span>
    <h2>Colour Usage Rules</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2 grid--gap-lg">
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DO</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Use Brand Yellow as the primary data series</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">When a single series is featured, use Brand Yellow (#FFCD00) with dark (#1E1E1E) text labels. It commands attention without overwhelming.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DON'T</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Don't use both brand colours as adjacent bars</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">Yellow and orange are too similar in lightness to be reliably distinguished side-by-side for multiple data series. Reserve them for primary/secondary roles separated by context.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DO</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Use Brand Orange for calls-to-action and highlights</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">Highlight a key data point, target line, or benchmark in Brand Orange. It creates urgency and draws the eye to the most important insight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DON'T</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Don't saturate the chart with brand colours</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">Using both brand colours throughout every element dilutes their impact. Majority of chart ink should be neutral. Brand colours earn attention through restraint.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DO</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Use light tints for area fills</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">For area charts, use the light tints (#FFF5CC, #FFE6D9) for fills, with the full brand colour on the stroke. This keeps charts readable and elegant.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-lg">
      <span class="kicker kicker--rule">DON'T</span>
      <h3 class="card-title-lg">Don't use brand colours for negative/positive coding</h3>
      <p class="section-intro">Avoid mapping Brand Orange to "bad" and Brand Yellow to "good" (or vice versa) — these aren't culturally neutral mappings. Use green/red for positive/negative, with brand colours for categorical distinctions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
